<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.5.4: Justifying Exponential Models</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01326</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.5.4: Justifying Exponential Models</md:title>
    <md:uuid>506aa52d-0310-42ca-add8-88ad6333b815</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">A patient who is diabetic receives 100 micrograms of insulin. The graph shows the amount of insulin, in micrograms,
  remaining in his bloodstream over time, in minutes.</para>
      <para id="para-00002">
        <figure id="fig-00001">
          <media alt="Graph of an exponential function, origin O. time (minutes) insulin (mg).">
            <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_07_09_01.svg"/>
          </media>
        </figure>
      </para>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00003">
        Scientists have found that the amount of insulin in a patient’s body changes exponentially. How can you
        check if the graph supports the scientists’ claim?
      </para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001"><para id="para-00004">Enter your answer here:</para><para id="para-00005">Compare your answer:</para>
    If the insulin decays exponentially, that means that after each minute, the same fraction of insulin will decay and
    remain. So one way to check if this prediction matches the data is to see what fraction of insulin decays (or
    remains) after the first minute, then after the second minute, and so on. If these fractions are the same, then the
    graph supports the scientists’ claim.
  
    If the insulin decays exponentially, that means that after each minute, the same fraction of insulin will decay and
    remain. So one way to check if this prediction matches the data is to see what fraction of insulin decays (or
    remains) after the first minute, then after the second minute, and so on. If these fractions are the same, then the
    graph supports the scientists’ claim.
  </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00006">Answer the following questions:
      </para>
          <para id="para-00007">
          How much insulin broke down in the first minute?
        </para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002"><para id="para-00008">Enter your answer here:</para><para id="para-00009">Compare your answer:</para>
    10 micrograms broke down.
  
    10 micrograms broke down.
  </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00010"> What fraction of the original insulin is that?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003"><para id="para-00011">Enter your answer here:</para><para id="para-00012">Compare your answer:</para>
    \(\frac{1}{10}\) of the initial injection
  
    \(\frac{1}{10}\) of the initial injection
  </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00013"> Answer the following questions. 
      </para>
          <para id="para-00014">
          How much insulin broke down in the second minute? </para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004"><para id="para-00015">Enter your answer here:</para><para id="para-00016">Compare your answer:</para>
    9 micrograms broke down.
  
    9 micrograms broke down.
  </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem id="prob-00005">
          <para id="para-00017"> What fraction is that of the amount one minute earlier?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00005"><para id="para-00018">Enter your answer here:</para><para id="para-00019">Compare your answer:</para>
    \(\frac{1}{10}\) of the amount one minute earlier
  
    \(\frac{1}{10}\) of the amount one minute earlier
  </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem id="prob-00006">
          <para id="para-00020">
        What fraction of insulin remains in the bloodstream for each minute that passes? Be prepared to show your
        reasoning.
      </para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00006">
          <para id="para-00021">Enter your answer here:</para>
          <para id="para-00022">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00023">\(\frac{9}{10}\). For example: In the next minute, another \(\frac{1}{10}\) of insulin broke down. Each time,
      \(\frac{9}{10}\) of the amount remains. </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <example id="exam-00001">
        <exercise>
          <problem id="prob-00007">
            <para id="para-00024">
        Complete the table to show the predicted amount of insulin 4 and 5 minutes after injection.</para>
            <table class="os-raise-horizontaltable" id="table-00001">
              <tgroup cols="7"><tbody><row>
                  <entry>
                    <emphasis>Time after Injection (Minutes)</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
            0
          </entry>
                  <entry>
            1
          </entry>
                  <entry>
            2
          </entry>
                  <entry>
            3
          </entry>
                  <entry>
            4
          </entry>
                  <entry>
            5
          </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>
                    <emphasis>Insulin in the Bloodstream (Micrograms)</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
            100
          </entry>
                  <entry>
            90
          </entry>
                  <entry>
            81
          </entry>
                  <entry>
            72.9
          </entry>
                  <entry> </entry>
                  <entry> </entry>
                </row>
              </tbody></tgroup></table>
            <para id="para-00025">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.
    </para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00007">
            <para id="para-00026">Compare your answers:</para>
            <table class="os-raise-horizontaltable" id="table-00002">
              <tgroup cols="7"><tbody><row>
                  <entry>
                    <emphasis>Time after Injection (Minutes)</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
          0
        </entry>
                  <entry>
          1
        </entry>
                  <entry>
          2
        </entry>
                  <entry>
          3
        </entry>
                  <entry>
          4
        </entry>
                  <entry>
          5
        </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>
                    <emphasis>Insulin in the Bloodstream (Micrograms)</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
          100
        </entry>
                  <entry>
          90
        </entry>
                  <entry>
          81
        </entry>
                  <entry>
          72.9
        </entry>
                  <entry>
          65.61
        </entry>
                  <entry>
          59.05
        </entry>
                </row>
              </tbody></tgroup></table>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem id="prob-00008">
          <para id="para-00027">Answer the following questions.
      </para>
          <para id="para-00028">
          Describe how you would find how many micrograms of insulin remain in his bloodstream after 10 minutes. 
        </para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00008">
          <para id="para-00029">Enter your answer here:</para>
          <para id="para-00030">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00031">Multiply 100 by \(\frac{9}{10}\) ten times: \(100 \cdot (\frac{9}{10})^{10}\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem id="prob-00009">
          <para id="para-00032">
            <para id="para-00033">
            After \(m\) minutes? </para>
          </para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00009">
          <para id="para-00034">Enter your answer here:</para>
          <para id="para-00035">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00036"> For example: Multiply 100 by \(\frac{9}{10}\) \(m\) times: \(100 \cdot (\frac{9}{10})^m\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
